{"name":"Breezy Touch","hex":"#c1d9e9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"breezy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["With a breeze blowing, with a lively wind, pleasantly windy.","With a cheerful, casual, lively and light-hearted manner."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A young woman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breezy"},{"term":"touch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Primarily physical senses.\n To make physical contact with; to bring the hand, finger or other part of the body into contact with. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","To make physical contact with; to bring the hand, finger or other part of the body into contact with. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of touching, especially with the hand or finger.","The faculty or sense of perception by physical contact."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/touch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Breezy","blocks":[{"text":"Breezy is a 1973 American romantic drama film directed by Clint Eastwood, produced by Robert Daley, and written by Jo Heims. The film stars William Holden and Kay Lenz, with Roger C. Carmel, Marj Dusay, and Joan Hotchkis in supporting roles. It is the third film directed by Eastwood and the first without him starring in it."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a7/Breezyfilmposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breezy"},{"title":"Somatosensory system","blocks":[{"text":"The somatosensory system, or somatic sensory system, is a subset of the sensory nervous system. The main functions of the somatosensory system are the perception of external stimuli, the perception of internal stimuli, and the regulation of body position and balance (proprioception). It is believed to act as a pathway between the different sensory modalities within the body."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Somatosensory_System.png/330px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Somatosensory_System.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somatosensory_system"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Tactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg/500px-Tactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Tactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg/500px-Tactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Touch is a crucial means of receiving information. This photo shows tactile markings identifying stairs for visually impaired people.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATactile_markings_stairs_for_visually_impaired.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png/500px-Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png/1280px-Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png/500px-Comprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This diagram linearly (unless otherwise mentioned) tracks the projections of all known structures that allow for touch to their relevant endpoints in the human brain.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComprehensive_List_of_Relevant_Pathways_for_the_Somatosensory_System.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Gray759.png/330px-Gray759.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Gray759.png/330px-Gray759.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gray's Anatomy, figure 759: the sensory tract, showing the pathway (blue) up the spinal cord, through the somatosensory thalamus, to S1 (Brodmann areas 3, 1, and 2), S2, and BA7","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray759.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Gray717.png/330px-Gray717.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Gray717.png/330px-Gray717.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gray's Anatomy, figure 717: detail showing path adjacent to the insular cortex (marked insula in this figure), adjacent to S1, S2, and BA7","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray717.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Tickle.jpg/500px-Tickle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Tickle.jpg/1280px-Tickle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Tickle.jpg/500px-Tickle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Touch can result in many different physiological reactions such as laughing at being tickled.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATickle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb